{% comment %}
  Kado Pixel — add to theme.liquid just before </body>
  Replace YOUR_API_URL and YOUR_PIXEL_KEY with actual values per client.
{% endcomment %}

<script>
  window.ADT_CONFIG = {
    apiUrl: 'YOUR_API_URL',
    pixelKey: 'YOUR_PIXEL_KEY'
  };
</script>
<script src="YOUR_API_URL/pixel.js" async></script>

{% comment %}
  If the customer is logged in, identify them automatically.
  This links returning customers to their ad history without waiting for checkout.
{% endcomment %}
{% if customer %}
<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    if (window.ADT) {
      ADT.identify('{{ customer.email | escape }}', { lead_type: 'logged_in' });
    }
  });
</script>
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  Product page view — fires ADT.trackViewContent with the product Shopify already
  knows about, rather than trying to infer it from the URL.
{% endcomment %}
{% if template contains 'product' %}
<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    if (window.ADT) {
      ADT.trackViewContent(
        { id: '{{ product.id }}', name: '{{ product.title | escape }}' },
        {{ product.price | money_without_currency | remove: ',' | default: 0 }}
      );
    }
  });
</script>
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  Add-to-cart and checkout-initiation used to be tracked here too (a generic
  /cart/add form-submit listener and a checkout-link click listener), running
  ALONGSIDE the Customer Events pixel below, which subscribes to Shopify's own
  product_added_to_cart/checkout_started events. Real production data (Nothing
  But Buckets, 2026-08-03) showed both firing for the same physical add-to-cart
  action a second or two apart — this snippet's copy always unnamed/$0 (it only
  ever had the cart form's variant id to work with, no title or price), the
  Customer Events copy always fully-formed. Every add-to-cart and checkout
  start was being double-counted. Removed here since Customer Events' own
  subscriptions are Shopify's platform-level event system, not theme markup
  the way this listener was — they fire reliably regardless of whether a theme
  submits a real form or calls Shopify's cart API directly via JavaScript, so
  nothing here needs to guess at page markup as a fallback anymore. See
  Settings → Customer events → Add custom pixel (the snippet below) for the
  one remaining source of these two events.
{% endcomment %}
